Usage: docker run ... <image> <command> [args]

Commands:
  decider                 run bin/domain-manager-workflow-poller (registers the SWF domain and
                          workflow types, then polls for decision tasks)
  activity-poller [N]     run bin/domain-manager-activity-poller --max-activity-workers N (default 4)
  config-standin <script> [port]
                          run the internal Config Service stand-in; mount standin-config-service.py
                          and standin_config_actions.py in and pass the script's container path
  exec <cmd> [args]       run an arbitrary command with the RM environment applied
  shell                   interactive bash with the RM environment applied

Environment:
  RM_EMULATOR_HOST       AWS emulator host reachable from this container. Not defaulted here on
                         purpose -- whether that is host.docker.internal, a --network host
                         loopback, or a compose service name depends on how you're running this,
                         and getting it wrong silently talks to the wrong thing. Set it to
                         override the endpoints already baked into the closure's stanza, or bake
                         a stanza with the right host in via a derived image.
  RM_CA_BUNDLE           path to the emulator's CA bundle (PEM; a bundle with the run CA appended
                         is expected, not a single certificate). Defaults to AWS_CA_BUNDLE, then
                         REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, so a harness that already exports either needs no
                         extra flag. Its certificates are MERGED at start-up into the trust stores
                         this image's interpreter actually consults -- which is NOT the same as
                         setting REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, see the trust block below. Not baked into the
                         image: the CA is regenerated on every emulator start, so a baked copy is
                         stale on the next run.
  RM_HEALTH_PORT         port for the `decider`/`activity-poller` liveness probe (default 8080) --
                         orchestrators that require a real HTTP GET readiness check (not a bare TCP
                         connect) poll this. Startup fails immediately if nothing is listening on it
                         a couple of seconds in, rather than deferring to a readiness timeout.
  RM_HEALTH_REQUIRED     1 (default) makes a failed liveness bind fatal; 0 downgrades it to a
                         warning, which is what a --network host run wants (the port is the host's,
                         so a collision with something already listening is routine).
  RM_SANDBOX_COMMON      where SearchServicesSandboxImageCommon was staged in this image
                         (default /opt/amazon/sandbox-common).
